522663 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc293ee1-edd2-5c82-9086-24fd0f998be3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522706 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522706 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522706 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522706 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ Either go to the movies, or you can watch a film at home.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522706 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22695eb6-0581-5f84-9a76-e047ac8b10b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522706 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Go to the movies.\n\n- $B:$ You can watch a film at home.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't go to the movies.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't go to the movies, then you can watch a film at home.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522709 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522709 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522709 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522709 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(7,\\theta )$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{9},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=13.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{n-x},\\,x=0,1,2,…,n.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522709 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ab1da93-7453-5e0d-90e1-e09775711415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522709 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=9$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=7$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{7-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,…,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{7-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{7-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{7-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{7-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (7-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,7n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{7n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=13$ and $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{13}(1-\\theta )^{7(9)-13} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{13}(1-\\theta )^{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522724 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522724 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522724 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522724 #edb/ref 1071 "In winter, the maximum daily temperature of a certain mountain location (rounded to the nearest degree) varies between $-{15}^{∘}C$ and ${2}^{∘}C,$ inclusive. Assuming that for a given winter day, each maximum temperature value between $-{15}^{∘}C$ and ${2}^{∘}C$ has an equal probability of occurring, what is the probability that the maximum daily temperature will reach at least ${0}^{∘}C$ on a randomly selected day?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522724 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b4a8271-3af9-5314-8310-6943f2c7643d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522724 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the maximum daily temperature (rounded to the nearest degree Celsius). From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{-15,-14,…,2\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\ge 0).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{-15,-14,…,2\\},$ and the set $S=\\{-15,-14,…,2\\}$ has $|S|=2-(-15)+1=18$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & x=-15,-14,…,2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\ge 0$ are $0,1,2.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 0)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522744 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522744 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522744 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522744 #edb/ref 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X>-15)$ can be approximated as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e38227f-eac1-53c7-9471-76f90491bdeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522744 #edb/ref 1074 "To apply the appropriate continuity correction, we first rewrite the strict inequality as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X>-15)=P(X\\ge -14)\n$$\n\nThen, we extend the endpoint of the interval $X\\ge -14$ by $0.5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge -14) & ≈P(Y\\ge -14-0.5) \\\\  & =P(Y\\ge -14.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522776 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522776 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522776 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522776 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $Γ(4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522776 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb7be639-e4f8-5a73-903b-c5d7e3e570a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522776 #edb/ref 1074 "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522816 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522816 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "023ef265-213e-5e4c-b092-5f0d563d74f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522816 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522818 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522818 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0450a032-0f52-5ba5-abe8-82e9866f5d76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522818 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522826 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522826 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "244586dd-a3f0-5297-a1fc-c393c1662ae7" #edb/ref 17592